      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nA product roadmap is supposed to be a compass. In practice, it usually becomes a contract—one that nobody intended to sign but everyone feels bound by. This tension between what a roadmap is meant to do and what it actually does explains why so many of them collapse under their own weight.\n\nThe first failure is one of certainty. Roadmaps are typically built as sequences of features with dates attached, presented with a confidence that the underlying knowledge does not justify. Teams commit to shipping Feature X in Q3 before anyone has validated that customers want Feature X, that it can be built in the time estimated, or that the market will still care by Q3. The document projects precision onto a fundamentally uncertain future. When reality diverges—as it always does—the roadmap either becomes a source of missed deadlines and eroded trust, or it is quietly abandoned. Either way, it has stopped functioning as a guide.\n\nThe second failure is that roadmaps are often lists of outputs rather than expressions of strategy. A good roadmap should answer *why*: what problem are we solving, for whom, and what will be different if we succeed? Most roadmaps instead answer *what*: a login redesign, a reporting dashboard, an integration with a popular tool. Stripped of intent, these items cannot be reprioritized intelligently. When a competitor moves or a customer churns, the team has no framework for deciding what to cut, because the roadmap never articulated what mattered most. It becomes a queue, and queues do not adapt.\n\nThird, roadmaps fail because they serve too many masters. Sales wants commitments to close deals. Executives want a narrative for the board. Engineering wants scope clarity. Customers want to know when their request will land. A single document straining to satisfy all of these becomes bloated, hedged, and ultimately incoherent. Features get added to placate a stakeholder rather than to advance the product, and the roadmap fills with items that exist for political rather than strategic reasons. Saying yes is easy; the roadmap becomes the graveyard of unspoken noes.\n\nFourth, roadmaps are rarely maintained as living documents. They are produced during planning season, circulated with fanfare, and then left to decay. Six months later, the team is working on things that never appeared on it, while items still listed have been silently deprioritized. The roadmap and the reality drift apart until the document is fiction. A roadmap that is not revisited regularly is not a plan—it is an artifact.\n\nUnderlying all of these is a deeper confusion: treating the roadmap as a promise rather than a hypothesis. The best product organizations frame their roadmaps around outcomes, express confidence honestly, keep near-term items specific and far-term items deliberately vague, and revise openly as they learn. They understand that the roadmap's value lies not in its accuracy but in the conversations it forces about priorities and trade-offs.\n\nMost roadmaps fail, then, not because planning is futile, but because they mistake the plan for the goal. A roadmap that cannot change is not a tool for navigation. It is a monument to what a team once believed.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack ideas, but because they mistake a roadmap for a promise rather than a learning tool. A roadmap is often presented as a confident sequence of features, dates, and milestones. In reality, product development takes place under uncertainty: customer needs evolve, technical constraints emerge, competitors respond, and business priorities shift. When a roadmap assumes certainty where none exists, it quickly becomes obsolete.\n\nOne common failure is feature-first planning. Teams fill roadmaps with outputs—new dashboards, integrations, mobile updates, or AI capabilities—without clearly connecting them to customer problems or measurable business outcomes. Shipping a feature does not guarantee that customers will use it, retain because of it, or pay for it. A roadmap that cannot explain why each initiative matters becomes a backlog with dates attached.\n\nAnother cause is poor customer understanding. Roadmaps are frequently driven by executive opinions, sales requests, loud customers, or competitor announcements rather than disciplined research. A single large prospect may demand a feature that distorts the product for everyone else. Meanwhile, quieter but more representative users may have entirely different needs. Without continuous discovery—interviews, behavioral data, usability testing, and market analysis—teams can build efficiently in the wrong direction.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they are treated as fixed commitments. Leadership may use them to reassure investors, coordinate departments, or forecast revenue. Sales teams may share them with prospects. Once dates become promises, product teams lose the ability to change course when evidence contradicts their assumptions. This creates a damaging incentive: deliver on time rather than deliver value. Teams may release incomplete, low-impact work simply to preserve the appearance of predictability.\n\nOrganizational misalignment compounds the problem. Product, engineering, design, marketing, sales, and customer success often operate with different goals and planning cycles. A roadmap may look coherent in a presentation but collapse in execution because the engineering effort is underestimated, go-to-market support is missing, or teams are competing for the same resources. Dependencies, technical debt, and operational constraints are often discovered too late.\n\nFinally, many roadmaps lack clear success measures. If a team launches an initiative without defining what success looks like—higher activation, reduced churn, faster task completion, increased revenue, or lower support volume—it cannot tell whether the work was worthwhile. The roadmap becomes a delivery calendar instead of a strategic instrument.\n\nEffective roadmaps do not eliminate uncertainty; they make uncertainty visible. They organize work around customer problems, desired outcomes, strategic bets, and learning milestones. They are updated as evidence changes. The best roadmap is therefore not the one that predicts the future most confidently, but the one that helps a company adapt to it most intelligently.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
